COMMIT 9c13830acd20fb59b10d8274e51aa338b42a65f0
test(bench): FIX-128 exercise a non-zero warmupFailed in the bench record

NodeBenchMainIT:72 asserted warmupFailed was 0 and nothing anywhere drove it
non-zero, so the field could have been hard-wired to 0 and the suite would not
have noticed. This is FIX-63's residual; its production half is already done and
counts correctly, so the new case is a guard rather than a red, and the hand-back
says that instead of describing a red that did not happen.

The case refuses the first 3 saves of a 5-message warm-up and asserts the record
reports exactly 3 -- a literal, not "greater than zero". The refusal is fault
injection at the store boundary, the pattern PgIntakeIT:306-315 already uses: a
sequence plus a plpgsql BEFORE INSERT trigger on `work`, which the intake's last
statement hits, so the save fails, Delivery turns it into a retryable Nack and
the harness counts a refusal. PREPARE truncates without dropping, and nextval is
not rolled back, so exactly the first 3 fail and all 3 fall in the warm-up. If
PREPARE ever dropped the trigger the case fails loudly -- last_value would read 1
instead of 45 -- rather than passing vacuously.

Acceptance 2 of my packet could not be met and the packet was wrong to ask for
it: the record does not carry `warmedUp`, which exists only as a local at
NodeBenchMain:314, so a value that is too LOW is invisible from outside. The dead
assertion the first round wrote over it -- assertEquals(2, warmup - warmupFailed)
-- recomputed production's own formula from two values pinned to literals two
lines above, so it could never fail while reading as coverage. It is deleted and a
comment says why. The observable part is checked instead: 2 + 40 messages and the
same number of step-0 bodies. The rest is FIX-134.

Evidence: VERIFIED_PASS, the whole class 12 run / 0 failed, BUILD SUCCESS
(agent/work/FIX-128-class.log) -- not a filtered run, because the earlier log
covered only 4 of the 12 cases. The mutant .put("warmupFailed", 0) at
NodeBenchMain:316 dies by assertion at NodeBenchMainIT:215, "expected <3> but was
<0>" (FIX-128-mutant-record0.log). A zero where the count is computed dies only by
the class @Timeout at 300 s. Both mutants ran in a scratch copy outside the
worktree, so no production file was edited by this task.

No coverage check is claimed for this task. A filtered -Pit run passes
-Djacoco.skip=true and is therefore no evidence at all about coverage; a test-only
addition cannot lower it, since JaCoCo counts production lines only, and the
integrated tree's own clean verify -Pit plus ci/coverage_check.py is the evidence.
That trap is now in agent/NOTES.md, together with the explanation of the 44 SEVERE
lines a passing verify -Pit prints: Deployments.java:649 logging every package a
test deliberately makes unrestorable.

